        $posts = [
            [
                'title' => 'OpenAI Slashes GPT-4o Pricing by 50% — Complete Developer Impact Analysis',
                'excerpt' => 'OpenAI has cut GPT-4o API costs in half, bringing input pricing to $2.50 per million tokens. We analyze what this means for developers, how it compares to Claude and Gemini, and whether you should switch providers.',
                'content' => "## What Happened\n\nOpenAI announced a major price reduction for **GPT-4o**, its flagship multimodal model. Effective immediately, input token costs dropped from \$5.00 to **\$2.50 per million tokens**, while output costs fell from \$15.00 to **\$10.00 per million**. This represents a 50% reduction on input and a 33% reduction on output.\n\nThe price cut applies to all API tiers — from individual developers on the free tier to enterprise customers with committed throughput. Batch API pricing was also reduced proportionally, bringing batch costs down to \$1.25/M input.\n\n## The Numbers at a Glance\n\n| Metric | Old Price | New Price | Change |\n|--------|----------|-----------|--------|\n| Input per 1M tokens | \$5.00 | \$2.50 | -50% |\n| Output per 1M tokens | \$15.00 | \$10.00 | -33% |\n| Batch input per 1M | \$2.50 | \$1.25 | -50% |\n| Context window | 128K | 128K | No change |\n| Max output tokens | 16K | 16K | No change |\n\n## Why OpenAI Cut Prices Now\n\nThis is not an isolated move. The AI API market is in a pricing war:\n\n1. **Google Gemini 2.5 Pro** launched at \$1.25/M input — half of GPT-4o's old price — and Google has been aggressively courting enterprise customers with free tiers and startup credits.\n\n2. **Anthropic Claude Sonnet 4** launched at \$3.00/M input with significantly improved coding benchmarks, putting pressure on OpenAI's premium positioning.\n\n3. **Open-source alternatives** hosted on Groq, Together AI, and Fireworks are undercutting everyone at \$0.20-0.90/M input for capable models like Llama 3.3 70B and DeepSeek-V3.\n\n4. **Enterprise adoption** is at an inflection point — companies are moving from experimentation to production, and cost is now the #1 decision factor.\n\nOpenAI is defending market share. The strategy is clear: make GPT-4o cheap enough that switching to a competitor isn't worth the engineering effort.\n\n## How It Compares to Competitors\n\nAt the new \$2.50/M input price, GPT-4o now sits in a competitive sweet spot:\n\n| Model | Input per 1M | Output per 1M | Context |\n|-------|-------------|---------------|---------|\n| **GPT-4o** | **\$2.50** | **\$10.00** | 128K |\n| Claude 3.5 Sonnet | \$3.00 | \$15.00 | 200K |\n| Claude Opus 4 | \$15.00 | \$75.00 | 200K |\n| Gemini 2.5 Pro | \$1.25 | \$10.00 | 1M |\n| Gemini 2.5 Flash | \$0.15 | \$0.60 | 1M |\n| DeepSeek-V3 | \$0.27 | \$1.10 | 128K |\n\nGPT-4o is now **cheaper than Claude 3.5 Sonnet** on input (\$2.50 vs \$3.00) and significantly cheaper on output (\$10.00 vs \$15.00). It is more expensive than Gemini 2.5 Pro (\$1.25/M) but many developers prefer GPT-4o for complex reasoning and instruction-following.\n\n## What This Means for Your Project\n\n### If You're on GPT-4o\n\nCongratulations — your API bill just dropped. For a project processing 10 million tokens per month (7M input, 3M output), the math is:\n\n- **Old cost**: (7 × \$5.00) + (3 × \$15.00) = \$35 + \$45 = **\$80/month**\n- **New cost**: (7 × \$2.50) + (3 × \$10.00) = \$17.50 + \$30 = **\$47.50/month**\n- **Savings**: \$32.50/month, or about **40%**\n\nAt scale (100M tokens/month), the savings become substantial — roughly \$400/month back in your budget.\n\n### If You're on Claude 3.5 Sonnet\n\nAt \$3.00/M input, Claude is now 20% more expensive than GPT-4o. Anthropic's value proposition rests on:\n\n- Superior instruction-following for complex multi-step tasks\n- Better safety and refusal handling\n- 200K context window (vs GPT-4o's 128K)\n\nIf your application relies heavily on long-context reasoning or requires Anthropic's specific safety features, the premium may be worth it. But for general-purpose chat, coding, and content generation, GPT-4o at \$2.50/M is now the better cost-to-performance ratio.\n\n### If You're on Open-Source Models\n\nOpen-source models via Groq, Together AI, and Fireworks remain the cheapest option at \$0.20-0.90/M. But the quality gap matters:\n\n- **Llama 3.3 70B** (\$0.59/M) is excellent for classification, summarization, and simple Q&A\n- **DeepSeek-V3** (\$0.27/M) punches above its weight on coding benchmarks\n- **GPT-4o at \$2.50/M** is still 4-12x more expensive than open-source\n\nThe decision: if your task is simple and high-volume, stick with open-source. If you need frontier-level reasoning, the GPT-4o premium at the new lower price is now much easier to justify.\n\n## Bottom Line\n\nOpenAI's price cut is great news for the entire ecosystem. It pressures Anthropic and Google to respond, which means even lower prices across the board. For developers, the practical advice is:\n\n1. **Re-run your cost projections** with the new GPT-4o pricing\n2. **Benchmark quality** — don't just chase the cheapest model; verify performance on YOUR use case\n3. **Use our cost calculator** to estimate exact monthly spend across any model combination\n\nThis pricing war is just getting started. We'll keep tracking every change.",
                'category' => 'pricing',
                'source_name' => 'OpenAI',
                'source_url' => 'https://openai.com/blog',
                'published_at' => now()->subDays(1),
